course: dictionary
lesson: 149
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word «affirm» is a good example. Read the whole sentence, then ask which meaning fits the clues around it.
explain: «The law in many countries lets people affirm instead of swearing, partly because of Quakers.» The words law and instead of swearing are the clues. Here affirm means to make a solemn promise to tell the truth in court without swearing on a holy book.
explain: Now try a different sentence: «Mrs Patel tries to affirm every child in her class with kind words.» Kind words and building children up are the clues, so affirm means to support and encourage them, showing they are valued. Always swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: To affirm something is to say firmly and clearly that it is true.
fact: To affirm a person is to support and encourage them, telling them they are valued.
fact: In law, to affirm is to make a solemn promise to tell the truth without swearing on a holy book.
fact: A higher court affirms a decision when it agrees that the decision was right.
fact: «Running an "if" backwards is a mistake grown-ups call affirming the consequent.» Phrases to remember: affirm the truth, affirm your faith, an affirmation.
exercise: «The law in many countries lets people affirm instead of swearing.» What does affirm mean here? | *To make a solemn promise to tell the truth without swearing on a holy book | To hide the truth from the judge | To buy a new suit for the trial | To shout very loudly in court
exercise: Coach Ruby says, «I affirm you: you are a valued member of this team.» What does affirm mean? | To say that something is false | To count the points | To cancel the match | *To support and encourage someone, showing they are valued
exercise: «The higher court affirms the judge's decision.» What does affirms mean in this sentence? | *Agrees that the decision was right | Asks for the case to be started again from the beginning | Forgets the decision | Changes the decision completely
test: «I will affirm that the team won fairly.» What does affirm mean? | To paint a picture of a game | To ask a question | To run away from a game | *To say firmly and clearly that it is true
test: In church, people affirm their faith by saying the creed. What does affirm your faith mean? | *To say firmly that you believe | To lose your faith | To draw a picture of a church | To hide in a church
exam: Which sentence uses affirm to mean support and encourage someone? | Kofi said he would affirm the truth in court | The judge will affirm the decision of the court | *Mrs Patel tries to affirm every child in her class with kind words
